# JCL - Complete Concepts & Examples Theory Guide

## Overview

**JCL (Job Control Language)** is the scripting language used on IBM mainframes (z/OS) to submit and execute programs, manage data sets (files), control job flow, and handle input/output operations.

---

## 1. JCL FUNDAMENTALS

### 1.1 Core Structure

JCL programs consist of three statement types:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│         JCL Job Structure           │
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│ JOB Statement (1)                   │
│ ├─ Defines job name, class, priority│
│ ├─ Sets accounting & notification   │
│ └─ One per job                      │
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│ EXEC Statement (1 or more)          │
│ ├─ Executes program or procedure    │
│ ├─ Sets program parameters          │
│ └─ Defines restart conditions       │
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│ DD Statement (Multiple)             │
│ ├─ Maps logical names to files      │
│ ├─ Specifies allocation parameters  │
│ └─ Manages data set disposition     │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### 1.2 Basic Syntax Rules

```
COLUMN STRUCTURE:
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 1-2   : // (statement identifier)                  │
│ 3-8   : Statement name (JOBNAME, STEPNAME, etc)    │
│ 9     : Blank separator                            │
│ 10-71 : Parameters                                 │
│ 72-80 : Sequence number (optional)                 │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

KEY RULES:
├─ Keywords: UPPERCASE required
├─ Parameters: Mixed case allowed
├─ Data: Any case allowed
├─ Continuation: New line with // in columns 1-2
├─ Comments: //* prefix
└─ Max line length: Column 71
```

### 1.3 Syntax Example

```jcl
//*==================================================
//* SIMPLE PAYROLL JOB
//*==================================================
//PAYROLL JOB (2450,TSK1),'MONTHLY PAY',
//            CLASS=A,
//            TIME=(0,30),
//            NOTIFY=&SYSUID
//*
//* Step 1: Calculate Payroll
//*
//CALC EXEC PGM=PAYROLL-CALC
//*
//SYSOUT DD SYSOUT=*
//EMPFILE DD DSN=HR.EMPLOYEES,DISP=SHR
//PAYFILE DD DSN=HR.PAYROLL.OUTPUT,
//            DISP=(NEW,CATLG),
//            SPACE=(CYL,(50,25))
//*
//
```

---

## 2. JOB STATEMENT - Job Control

### 2.1 Purpose & Theory

The **JOB** statement is the first statement that tells the OS:
- Who submitted the job (user ID)
- Job priority (CLASS)
- Resource requirements (TIME, REGION)
- Output routing (MSGCLASS)
- Accounting information
- How to notify completion

### 2.2 Full Syntax

```
//jobname JOB (acct,subacct),'Description',
//            REGION=memory,
//            TIME=(minutes,seconds),
//            CLASS=priority-class,
//            MSGCLASS=output-class,
//            MSGLEVEL=(job,proc),
//            NOTIFY=userid,
//            TYPRUN=runtype
```

### 2.3 Parameters Explained

```
(2450,TSK1)          Accounting Information
  ├─ First: Account number (cost center)
  ├─ Second: Project/task code
  └─ For billing and cost tracking

'MONTHLY PAYROLL'    Job Description (max 20 chars)
  ├─ Visible in output
  ├─ Should be meaningful
  └─ For job identification

REGION=8192K         Memory Allocation
  ├─ K=Kilobytes, M=Megabytes
  ├─ Typical: 4M-32M
  ├─ Too small → ABEND (abnormal end)
  └─ Too large → System congestion

TIME=(0,30)          CPU Time Limit
  ├─ Format: (minutes, seconds)
  ├─ 0 = Unlimited (if allowed)
  ├─ (0,30) = 30 seconds
  ├─ (10,0) = 10 minutes
  └─ Exceeded → Job cancelled

CLASS=A              Job Scheduling Class
  ├─ Range: A-Z, 0-9
  ├─ A = High priority (usually)
  ├─ Z = Low priority
  └─ Determines when job executes

MSGCLASS=X           Message/Output Class
  ├─ Range: A-Z, 0-9
  ├─ Determines output queue
  ├─ Different classes = different routing
  └─ System-dependent

MSGLEVEL=(1,1)       Message Level
  ├─ Format: (JOB statements, PROC expansion)
  ├─ 0 = Nothing
  ├─ 1 = Statements + messages
  └─ 2 = All including PROC details

NOTIFY=USERID        Completion Notification
  ├─ Email/message when job done
  ├─ &SYSUID = Current user (special var)
  └─ Useful for monitoring

TYPRUN=HOLD          Type of Run
  ├─ SCAN = Check syntax only
  ├─ HOLD = Hold in queue before exec
  ├─ COPY = Produce execution JCL only
  └─ Default: Normal execution
```

### 2.4 Common Job Classes

```
CLASS    Priority   Typical Use
────────────────────────────────────
A        High       Critical batch jobs
B        Med-High   Important daily jobs
C        Medium     Standard batch jobs
D        Low        Maintenance jobs
Z        Lowest     Archival, cleanup

Financial Systems:  Usually CLASS=A
Standard Business:  Usually CLASS=B,C
Maintenance:        Usually CLASS=D,Z
```

### 2.5 JOB Statement Examples

```jcl
//* Simple job
//SIMPLE JOB (1234)

//* Complete job with all parameters
//COMPLETE JOB (5000,PAYROLL),'WEEKLY PAYROLL',
//              REGION=16M,
//              TIME=(0,120),
//              CLASS=A,
//              MSGCLASS=X,
//              MSGLEVEL=(1,1),
//              NOTIFY=PAYROLL-OPS,
//              TYPRUN=HOLD

//* High-priority critical job
//CRITICAL JOB (9999,CRITICAL),'CRITICAL PROCESS',
//              REGION=64M,
//              TIME=(10,0),
//              CLASS=A,
//              NOTIFY=&SYSUID

//* Low-priority cleanup
//CLEANUP JOB (1000,MAINT),'CLEANUP OLD DATA',
//            CLASS=Z,
//            REGION=2M
```

---

## 3. EXEC STATEMENT - Program Execution

### 3.1 Purpose & Theory

The **EXEC** statement tells OS what to execute:
- Which program to run (PGM=)
- Which procedure to execute (PROC=)
- Parameters to pass to program
- Restart conditions
- Step-specific resources
- Conditional execution

### 3.2 Full Syntax

```
//stepname EXEC PGM=program-name,
//              PARM='parameters',
//              REGION=memory,
//              TIME=(minutes,seconds),
//              COND=(return-code,operator)

OR

//stepname EXEC PROC=procedure-name,
//              PARAM1=value1,
//              PARAM2=value2
```

### 3.3 Parameters Explained

```
PGM=PROGRAM-NAME     Execute Program
  ├─ Full program name
  ├─ Example: PGM=PAYROLL-CALC
  ├─ Or: PGM=SYS1.SORTLIB(SORT)
  └─ Cannot use with PROC=

PROC=PROCEDURE-NAME  Execute Procedure
  ├─ Predefined procedure name
  ├─ Must exist in PROCLIB
  ├─ Cannot use with PGM=
  └─ Example: PROC=PAYROLL-PROCESS

PARM='parameters'    Pass Parameters to Program
  ├─ Max 100 characters
  ├─ COBOL: Via LINKAGE SECTION
  ├─ PL/I: Via parameter passing
  └─ Example: PARM='/DEBUG /VERBOSE'

REGION=memory        Memory for this Step
  ├─ Overrides JOB REGION
  ├─ Each step can have different size
  ├─ Example: REGION=8192K
  └─ For step-specific needs

TIME=(min,sec)       Time Limit for this Step
  ├─ Can differ from JOB TIME
  ├─ (0,0) = No limit (if allowed)
  ├─ Applied only to this step
  └─ Useful for variable-duration steps

COND=(rc,op)         Condition to Execute Step
  ├─ Skip if condition TRUE
  ├─ rc = return code to compare
  ├─ op = Operator (EQ,NE,LT,LE,GT,GE)
  └─ Explained in detail below
```

### 3.4 Return Codes & COND Logic

```
RETURN CODE CONVENTION:
┌─────────────────┬────────────────────────┐
│ Return Code (RC)│ Meaning                │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────┤
│ 0               │ Success                │
│ 4               │ Warning (non-critical) │
│ 8               │ Error                  │
│ 12              │ Serious error          │
│ 16              │ Critical error         │
│ 20+             │ System/Program error   │
└─────────────────┴────────────────────────┘

COBOL CODE:
  MOVE 0 TO RETURN-CODE.   (success)
  MOVE 4 TO RETURN-CODE.   (warning)
  MOVE 8 TO RETURN-CODE.   (error)

COND LOGIC EXPLAINED:

COND=(0,NE)  ← Skip if return ≠ 0 (NE = Not Equal)
  ├─ If RC = 0: Condition TRUE → SKIP step
  ├─ If RC ≠ 0: Condition FALSE → EXECUTE step
  └─ Use: Run step only if PREVIOUS FAILED

COND=(0,EQ)  ← Skip if return = 0 (EQ = Equal)
  ├─ If RC = 0: Condition TRUE → SKIP step
  ├─ If RC ≠ 0: Condition FALSE → EXECUTE step
  └─ Use: Run step only if PREVIOUS SUCCEEDED

COND=(4,LT)  ← Skip if return < 4 (LT = Less Than)
  ├─ If RC < 4: Condition TRUE → SKIP step
  ├─ If RC ≥ 4: Condition FALSE → EXECUTE step
  └─ Use: Run step only for serious errors (≥4)

COND=(8,LE)  ← Skip if return ≤ 8 (LE = Less Than or Equal)
  ├─ If RC ≤ 8: Condition TRUE → SKIP step
  ├─ If RC > 8: Condition FALSE → EXECUTE step
  └─ Use: Run step only for critical errors (>8)

COND=(16,GE) ← Skip if return ≥ 16 (GE = Greater Than or Equal)
  ├─ If RC ≥ 16: Condition TRUE → SKIP step
  ├─ If RC < 16: Condition FALSE → EXECUTE step
  └─ Use: Run step only if fatal error
```

### 3.5 EXEC Examples

```jcl
//* Simple execution
//STEP1 EXEC PGM=PAYROLL-CALC

//* With parameters
//STEP2 EXEC PGM=DATA-EXTRACT,
//           PARM='DEBUG MODE=FULL /VERBOSE'

//* Execute procedure
//STEP3 EXEC PROC=BACKUP-PROCEDURE

//* Conditional execution (skip if success)
//STEP4 EXEC PGM=VALIDATION,COND=(0,NE)
  └─ Only run if previous step had errors

//* Conditional execution (run if success)
//STEP5 EXEC PGM=NEXT-PROCESS,COND=(0,EQ)
  └─ Only run if previous step succeeded

//* With custom resources
//STEP6 EXEC PGM=BIG-CALCULATION,
//           REGION=32M,
//           TIME=(5,0)
  └─ 32MB memory, 5 minutes CPU time

//* Multiple conditions
//STEP7 EXEC PGM=ERROR-HANDLER,
//           COND=((0,NE,STEP1),(4,LT,STEP2))
  └─ Skip if STEP1 ok AND STEP2 has no error
```

---

## 4. DD STATEMENT - Data Set Definition

### 4.1 Purpose & Theory

The **DD** (Data Definition) statement:
- Maps logical names to physical files
- Allocates storage space
- Defines record format and attributes
- Manages data set disposition (keep/delete)
- Routes input/output (SYSOUT, SYSIN)

### 4.2 Full Syntax

```
//ddname DD DSN=dataset.name,
//           DISP=(status,after-normal,after-abnormal),
//           UNIT=device,
//           VOL=SER=volser,
//           SPACE=(unit,(primary,secondary),release),
//           LRECL=length,
//           RECFM=format,
//           BLKSIZE=size
```

### 4.3 DD Parameters Explained

```
DDNAME (Columns 3-8)
  ├─ Logical data set name
  ├─ Max 8 characters
  ├─ COBOL: SELECT...ASSIGN TO ddname
  ├─ Examples: SYSOUT, SYSIN, INFILE, OUTFILE
  └─ Standard names: SYSOUT, SYSIN, SYSPRINT

DSN=dataset.name     Physical Dataset Name
  ├─ Hierarchical structure with periods
  ├─ Max 44 characters total
  ├─ Each segment max 8 characters
  ├─ Format: COMPANY.APP.TYPE.VERSION
  ├─ Example: PAYROLL.EMPLOYEES.MASTER.PROD
  ├─ Quotes if mixed case: DSN='MyData'
  └─ & for substitution: DSN=PAYROLL.&ENV.DATA

DISP=(status,normal,abnormal)
  ├─ Data Set DIsPosition
  │
  ├─ STATUS (what exists):
  │  ├─ NEW = Creating new dataset
  │  ├─ OLD = Existing (exclusive access)
  │  ├─ SHR = Shared (read-only)
  │  └─ MOD = Modify (append data)
  │
  ├─ AFTER-NORMAL (on success):
  │  ├─ KEEP = Keep dataset after job
  │  ├─ DELETE = Delete after job
  │  ├─ CATLG = Catalog for future use
  │  └─ UNCATLG = Remove from catalog
  │
  └─ AFTER-ABNORMAL (on failure):
     ├─ DELETE = Delete if job fails
     ├─ KEEP = Keep if job fails
     ├─ CATLG = Catalog even if fails
     └─ UNCATLG = Uncatalog even if fails

  Examples:
    DISP=(NEW,CATLG,DELETE)   New, keep, delete if fails
    DISP=(OLD,KEEP,KEEP)      Existing, keep regardless
    DISP=(SHR)                Read-only shared
    DISP=(MOD)                Append to existing

UNIT=device          Hardware Device Type
  ├─ SYSDA = Direct access (disk)
  ├─ TAPE = Tape drive
  ├─ 3390 = Disk model 3390
  ├─ 3480 = Tape cartridge 3480
  └─ Default: Usually SYSDA

VOL=SER=volser       Volume Serial Number
  ├─ Specific storage volume
  ├─ VOL=SER=DISK01 = Use DISK01 volume
  ├─ VOL=SER=TAPE03 = Use TAPE03 volume
  └─ Only if specific volume required

SPACE=(unit,(primary,secondary),release)
  ├─ Storage space allocation
  │
  ├─ UNIT:
  │  ├─ CYL = Cylinder (500KB on 3390)
  │  ├─ TRK = Track (~14KB)
  │  └─ BLK = Blocks (variable size)
  │
  ├─ PRIMARY = Initial allocation
  │  └─ SPACE=(CYL,(100,...) = 100 cylinders
  │
  ├─ SECONDARY = Extents if needed
  │  └─ SPACE=(...,50) = Up to 50 additional
  │
  └─ RELEASE:
     ├─ RLSE = Release unused at close
     ├─ No release = Keep allocation
     └─ RLSE saves space

  Example: SPACE=(CYL,(100,50),RLSE)
    ├─ 100 cylinders primary
    ├─ 50 cylinders secondary
    ├─ Release unused space

LRECL=length         Logical Record Length
  ├─ Max bytes per record
  ├─ Must match program FD
  ├─ Example: LRECL=120 (120-byte records)
  ├─ LRECL=* (for variable length)
  └─ LRECL=0 (for undefined)

RECFM=format         Record Format
  ├─ F = Fixed length
  ├─ V = Variable length
  ├─ U = Undefined
  ├─ B = Blocked (second character)
  ├─ A = EBCDIC text (third character)
  │
  ├─ Common:
  │  ├─ RECFM=FB = Fixed Blocked (most common)
  │  ├─ RECFM=F = Fixed, unblocked
  │  ├─ RECFM=VB = Variable Blocked
  │  └─ RECFM=V = Variable unblocked
  │
  └─ COBOL match: 
     └─ FB: 120-byte records → LRECL=120

BLKSIZE=size         Block Size (bytes)
  ├─ Records per physical block
  ├─ Should be multiple of LRECL
  ├─ Larger = Better I/O performance
  ├─ Example: BLKSIZE=6000 (50 × 120-byte records)
  ├─ Balance with memory
  └─ Typical: 3-10 records per block

DCB=(...)           Data Control Block
  ├─ Alternative syntax for combining:
  ├─ DCB=(LRECL=80,RECFM=FB,BLKSIZE=6000)
  └─ Used instead of individual parameters

SYSOUT=*            System Output (for printing)
  ├─ * = Default output device (printer)
  ├─ A,B,C = Different output classes
  ├─ Combined with MSGCLASS
  └─ Used for printed reports

DUMMY               Dummy Dataset (discards output)
  ├─ Useful for testing
  ├─ Useful for optional input
  ├─ Discards all written data
  └─ DD DUMMY (no parameters)
```

